#module "tasks"
'' std/tasks: channel -- tasks.Channel( of T ), a typed thread-safe queue
''
'' RFC-0002 (C:\dev\docs\goroutines\RFC-0002-channels.md).  Go's hchan,
'' transliterated: a lock, a ring buffer, two waiter lists, and a direct
'' handoff path that bypasses the buffer.
''
''     dim ch as Channel( of long ) = Channel( of long )( 16 )
''     ...
''     while ch.Receive( v )        '' ends when the producer closes and drains
''         Process( v )
''     wend
''
'' CAPACITY 0 IS A RENDEZVOUS, not a zero-length buffer.  Send blocks until a
'' receiver takes the value, and the value is handed to the receiver DIRECTLY
'' rather than through the buffer.  That is what makes the rendezvous an
'' ordering point as well as a handoff, and implementing it as a one-element
'' buffer -- which passes every round-trip test -- silently destroys the
'' guarantee (RFC-0002 section 4).
''
'' COPIES SHARE.  A Channel is a refcounted handle to a channel, not a channel.
'' Handing a copy to each of four workers gives four handles on ONE queue, which
'' is what makes fan-out work.  Deliberately unlike containers.Array, whose copy is
'' deep.  The destructor does NOT Close: closing means "no more values will be
'' sent", and a scope that merely stopped holding a reference has not said that.
''
'' A DEFAULT-CONSTRUCTED CHANNEL IS NIL, and a nil channel blocks forever on
'' Send and Receive.  That is Go's rule and it looks like a defect until SELECT
'' arrives, where assigning a nil channel is how a case is disabled.  IsNil( )
'' exists so a program can assert its own initialisation, because "my program
'' hangs" is the symptom of forgetting it.
''
'' NO MEMBER MAY CONSTRAIN T.  Every member body of a generic is replayed for
'' every instantiation whether or not it is called, so a member needing '=' on T
'' would make the whole type unusable for a T without one -- the rule Optional
'' and Array already follow.  Nothing here uses more than default construction,
'' copy assignment and destruction.
''
'' REQUIRES -mt.  Without it the program links the single-threaded runtime and
'' the results are undefined, so this refuses to compile rather than corrupt.


#if not defined( __FB_MT__ )
    #error "std/tasks requires -mt (the multithreaded runtime)"
#endif

#include once "fberror.bi"
#include once "fbpark.bi"
#include once "fbclaim.bi"

using FB

'' ------------------------------------------------------------------ waiter
''
'' RFC-0002 section 5.  A waiter record is owned by the WAITING THREAD and
'' lives in its frame; the channel's queues hold pointers to records they do
'' not own.  That inversion is the whole of what makes SELECT addable later:
'' one waiter can appear in N channels' queues.  Parking waiters on a
'' channel-owned condition variable would pass every test in phase 2 and make
'' phase 5 a rewrite.
''
'' 'slot' points at the counterparty's storage -- the sender's value, or the
'' receiver's output variable -- so a handoff is one copy with no staging.
'' The NON-GENERIC half of a wait record.  It is the first field of
'' ChanWaiter( of T ), so a 'ChanWaiterCore ptr' and a 'ChanWaiter( of T ) ptr'
'' are the same address and either can be cast to the other.
''
'' This split is what SELECT needs and is the reason RFC-0002 section 5 made the
'' waiter discipline normative in phase 2.  One SELECT registers ONE record in
'' the queues of several channels, and those channels have different element
'' types -- so the queue links, the parker and the claim flag cannot live in a
'' type that mentions T.  Only the value slot can.
public type ChanWaiterCore
    as any ptr                parker
    as boolean                claimed
    as boolean                closedwake
    as ChanWaiterCore ptr     prevw
    as ChanWaiterCore ptr     nextw

    '' SELECT only.  NULL for a plain Send or Receive.
    ''
    '' 'seldone' points at the owning select's single done flag, and claiming has
    '' to go through THAT rather than through this record's own 'claimed' -- the
    '' claim is per-SELECT, not per-record.  Two different channels can reach two
    '' different records of the same select at the same moment, and if each only
    '' checked its own record both would win and the select would fire twice.
    '' This is what Go's compare-and-swap on g.selectDone is for.
    ''
    '' 'caseidx' lets the woken select report which case fired without the waker
    '' having to know anything about the select's layout.
    as boolean ptr            seldone
    as long                   caseidx
end type

public type ChanWaiter( of T )
    as ChanWaiterCore         c
    as T ptr                  slot
end type

'' ------------------------------------------------------------------- state
''
'' One allocation, shared by every handle, freed when the last one dies.
'' 'buf' is an ordinary FreeBASIC dynamic array so the language does element
'' construction, copying and destruction -- the same choice Array made, and the
'' reason there is no manual element lifetime to get wrong.
'' Likewise the state.  Everything SELECT touches without knowing T -- the lock,
'' the counts, the closed flag, the two waiter queues -- is in ChanCore, which is
'' the first field, so a channel can be locked and polled through a 'ChanCore
'' ptr' obtained from any Channel( of T ).
public type ChanCore
    as any ptr                lok
    as long                   cap
    as long                   num
    as long                   head
    as long                   tail
    as boolean                isclosed
    as long                   refs
    as ChanWaiterCore ptr     sfirst
    as ChanWaiterCore ptr     slast
    as ChanWaiterCore ptr     rfirst
    as ChanWaiterCore ptr     rlast
end type

type ChanState( of T )
    as ChanCore               core
    as T                      buf( any )
end type

'' ---------------------------------------------------------------- iterator
''
'' Value( ) is BYVAL: the item lives in the iterator and the next MoveNext
'' overwrites it, so a byref binding would alias a slot about to be reused.
'' 'for each byref v in ch' is therefore a compile error, through the existing
'' FB_ERRMSG_FOREACHNOBYREF diagnostic rather than anything added here.
public type ChannelIterator( of T )
    as any ptr   src
    as T         cur
    as boolean   ok
    declare function IsValid( ) as boolean
    declare function Value( ) as T
    declare sub MoveNext( )
end type

public type Channel( of T )
    private:
    as any ptr st

    public:
    declare constructor( )
    declare constructor( byval bufcap as long )
    declare constructor( byref rhs as Channel( of T ) )
    declare destructor( )
    declare operator let( byref rhs as Channel( of T ) )

    declare function Send      ( byref v   as T ) as boolean
    declare function Receive   ( byref out as T ) as boolean
    declare function TrySend   ( byref v   as T ) as boolean
    declare function TryReceive( byref out as T ) as boolean
    declare function SendFor   ( byref v   as T, byval ms as ulong ) as boolean
    declare function ReceiveFor( byref out as T, byval ms as ulong ) as boolean

    declare sub      Close( )
    declare function IsClosed( ) as boolean
    declare function IsNil( ) as boolean
    declare function Count( ) as long
    declare function Capacity( ) as long

    declare function GetIterator( ) as ChannelIterator( of T )
    '' The shared state, opaque, for SELECT and the channel helpers: read-only, because
    '' the field is private (review LIB-04). NULL for a nil channel.
    declare function Handle( ) as any ptr

    '' NEVER CALL THIS.  It exists so that generated code can SPELL the element
    '' type, as 'typeof( *ch.ElemPtr( ) )'.  A generic instantiation prints as a
    '' mangled internal name and is not writable as source, so the SELECT
    '' statement has no other way to declare a variable of the element type --
    '' which it must do, because inferring T from both the channel and the value
    '' of 'case send 7 to ch' conflicts (long against integer).
    ''
    '' 'typeof' does not evaluate its argument, so the call never happens and the
    '' body below is unreachable in generated code.
    declare function ElemPtr( ) as T ptr

    '' internal
    declare function hWait( byref w as ChanWaiter( of T ), byval issend as boolean, _
                            byval timed as boolean, byval ms as ulong ) as boolean
end type

'' ------------------------------------------------------------- queue plumbing
''
'' Free procedures rather than members of ChanState so that the state type stays
'' a plain record.  All of them are called with the channel lock HELD.

sub hPushSend( byval s as ChanCore ptr, byval w as ChanWaiterCore ptr )
    w->prevw = s->slast
    w->nextw = nullptr
    if( s->slast ) then s->slast->nextw = w else s->sfirst = w
    s->slast = w
end sub

sub hPushRecv( byval s as ChanCore ptr, byval w as ChanWaiterCore ptr )
    w->prevw = s->rlast
    w->nextw = nullptr
    if( s->rlast ) then s->rlast->nextw = w else s->rfirst = w
    s->rlast = w
end sub

'' Unlink a record from whichever queue holds it.  O(list), and required: a
'' waiter that gave up -- a timeout, or another SELECT case firing -- must be
'' able to remove itself (RFC-0002 section 5, invariant 4).
sub hUnlink( byval s as ChanCore ptr, byval w as ChanWaiterCore ptr, byval issend as boolean )
    if( w->prevw ) then w->prevw->nextw = w->nextw
    if( w->nextw ) then w->nextw->prevw = w->prevw
    if( issend ) then
        if( s->sfirst = w ) then s->sfirst = w->nextw
        if( s->slast  = w ) then s->slast  = w->prevw
    else
        if( s->rfirst = w ) then s->rfirst = w->nextw
        if( s->rlast  = w ) then s->rlast  = w->prevw
    end if
    w->prevw = nullptr
    w->nextw = nullptr
end sub

'' Claim a record, i.e. win the right to wake it.  Returns FALSE if somebody
'' else already has it.
''
'' THE CLAIM LOCK IS PROCESS-WIDE, and it has to be.  Through phase 2 a waiter
'' was only ever in ONE channel's queue, so the channel's own lock protected the
'' flag.  A SELECT puts one record in several queues, so two threads holding two
'' DIFFERENT channel locks can reach the same record; nothing either of them
'' holds excludes the other.  Go does this with a compare-and-swap and
'' FreeBASIC has no atomics, so it is a mutex -- held across a read and a write
'' of one boolean, never across a park, an allocation or another lock.
''
'' Lock order: channel lock first, then this.  Never the reverse.
function hClaim( byval w as ChanWaiterCore ptr ) as boolean
    dim as boolean won = any
    ClaimLock( )
    if( w->seldone ) then
        '' a SELECT record: the whole select fires at most once
        won = ( *(w->seldone) = FALSE )
        if( won ) then
            *(w->seldone) = TRUE
            w->claimed = TRUE      '' so the select can find WHICH case won
        end if
    else
        won = ( w->claimed = FALSE )
        if( won ) then w->claimed = TRUE
    end if
    ClaimUnlock( )
    return won
end function

'' Pop the first waiter that can still be claimed, claiming it.  Records that
'' another channel already claimed are dropped from this queue on the way past:
'' they are dead to this channel, and leaving them would make every later
'' operation walk them again.
function hPopSend( byval s as ChanCore ptr ) as ChanWaiterCore ptr
    dim as ChanWaiterCore ptr w = s->sfirst
    while( w )
        dim as ChanWaiterCore ptr nxt = w->nextw
        hUnlink( s, w, TRUE )
        if( hClaim( w ) ) then
            return w
        end if
        w = nxt
    wend
    return nullptr
end function

function hPopRecv( byval s as ChanCore ptr ) as ChanWaiterCore ptr
    dim as ChanWaiterCore ptr w = s->rfirst
    while( w )
        dim as ChanWaiterCore ptr nxt = w->nextw
        hUnlink( s, w, FALSE )
        if( hClaim( w ) ) then
            return w
        end if
        w = nxt
    wend
    return nullptr
end function

'' ------------------------------------------------ exposed for SELECT
''
'' SELECT manipulates a channel's queues without knowing its element type, which
'' is exactly what the ChanCore/ChanWaiterCore split is for.  These are thin
'' public names over the plumbing above; they assume the channel's lock is HELD.

sub ChanPushSend( byval c as ChanCore ptr, byval w as ChanWaiterCore ptr )
    hPushSend( c, w )
end sub

sub ChanPushRecv( byval c as ChanCore ptr, byval w as ChanWaiterCore ptr )
    hPushRecv( c, w )
end sub

sub ChanUnlink( byval c as ChanCore ptr, byval w as ChanWaiterCore ptr, byval issend as boolean )
    hUnlink( c, w, issend )
end sub

'' A nil channel blocks forever on Send and Receive.  Implemented rather than
'' faked, because SELECT relies on a nil case simply never becoming ready.
sub hBlockForever( )
    dim as any ptr p = ParkerSelf( )
    do
        Park( p )
    loop
end sub

'' ------------------------------------------------------------- construction

constructor Channel( of T )( )
    this.st = nullptr                     '' nil channel
end constructor

constructor Channel( of T )( byval bufcap as long )
    if( bufcap < 0 ) then
        error( FB.FB_RTERROR_ILLEGALFUNCTIONCALL )
        this.st = nullptr
        exit constructor
    end if

    dim as ChanState( of T ) ptr s = new ChanState( of T )
    s->core.lok  = MutexCreate( )
    s->core.cap  = bufcap
    s->core.num  = 0
    s->core.head = 0
    s->core.tail = 0
    s->core.isclosed = FALSE
    s->core.refs = 1
    if( bufcap > 0 ) then
        redim s->buf( 0 to bufcap-1 )
    end if
    this.st = s
end constructor

constructor Channel( of T )( byref rhs as Channel( of T ) )
    this.st = rhs.st
    if( this.st ) then
        dim as ChanState( of T ) ptr s = cptr( ChanState( of T ) ptr, this.st )
        MutexLock( s->core.lok )
        s->core.refs += 1
        MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
    end if
end constructor

'' Does NOT close.  Frees the state only when the last handle dies, and the
'' dynamic array destroys every slot it constructed -- including items still
'' buffered at that point, exactly once.
destructor Channel( of T )( )
    if( this.st = nullptr ) then exit destructor

    dim as ChanState( of T ) ptr s = cptr( ChanState( of T ) ptr, this.st )
    MutexLock( s->core.lok )
    s->core.refs -= 1
    dim as boolean dead = ( s->core.refs = 0 )
    MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )

    if( dead ) then
        MutexDestroy( s->core.lok )
        delete s
    end if
    this.st = nullptr
end destructor

operator Channel( of T ).let( byref rhs as Channel( of T ) )
    if( this.st = rhs.st ) then exit operator

    '' release the old handle first, then take the new one
    if( this.st ) then
        dim as ChanState( of T ) ptr s = cptr( ChanState( of T ) ptr, this.st )
        MutexLock( s->core.lok )
        s->core.refs -= 1
        dim as boolean dead = ( s->core.refs = 0 )
        MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
        if( dead ) then
            MutexDestroy( s->core.lok )
            delete s
        end if
    end if

    this.st = rhs.st
    if( this.st ) then
        dim as ChanState( of T ) ptr s2 = cptr( ChanState( of T ) ptr, this.st )
        MutexLock( s2->core.lok )
        s2->core.refs += 1
        MutexUnlock( s2->core.lok )
    end if
end operator

'' ------------------------------------------------------------------ queries

function Channel( of T ).Handle( ) as any ptr
    return this.st
end function

function Channel( of T ).IsNil( ) as boolean
    return this.st = nullptr
end function

function Channel( of T ).IsClosed( ) as boolean
    if( this.st = nullptr ) then return FALSE
    dim as ChanState( of T ) ptr s = cptr( ChanState( of T ) ptr, this.st )
    MutexLock( s->core.lok )
    dim as boolean r = s->core.isclosed
    MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
    return r
end function

'' A snapshot, stale the instant it returns, like every such query in a
'' concurrent system.  Metrics only -- never control flow.
function Channel( of T ).Count( ) as long
    if( this.st = nullptr ) then return 0
    dim as ChanState( of T ) ptr s = cptr( ChanState( of T ) ptr, this.st )
    MutexLock( s->core.lok )
    dim as long r = s->core.num
    MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
    return r
end function

function Channel( of T ).Capacity( ) as long
    if( this.st = nullptr ) then return 0
    dim as ChanState( of T ) ptr s = cptr( ChanState( of T ) ptr, this.st )
    return s->core.cap
end function

'' --------------------------------------------------------------------- wait
''
'' Enqueue, drop the lock, park, then re-take the lock and report whether we
'' were claimed.  Returns TRUE if a counterparty claimed this record (the value
'' has already been moved through 'slot'), FALSE on timeout.
''
'' No stale-permit loop is needed, and that is a property of the protocol
'' rather than luck: a waker CLAIMS under the lock before it unparks, and this
'' function checks 'claimed' under the lock.  So an unpark without a claim
'' cannot happen, and a park cannot miss one -- the permit is sticky
'' (RFC-0001 section 3).
function Channel( of T ).hWait( byref w as ChanWaiter( of T ), byval issend as boolean, _
                                byval timed as boolean, byval ms as ulong ) as boolean
    dim as ChanState( of T ) ptr s = cptr( ChanState( of T ) ptr, this.st )

    if( timed ) then
        ParkTimed( w.c.parker, ms )
    else
        Park( w.c.parker )
    end if

    MutexLock( s->core.lok )
    dim as boolean got = w.c.claimed
    if( got = FALSE ) then
        '' timed out, or spuriously woken: nobody has touched this record, so
        '' removing it here means no waker can ever reach it afterwards
        hUnlink( @s->core, @w.c, issend )
    end if
    MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )

    return got
end function

'' --------------------------------------------------------------------- send

function Channel( of T ).Send( byref v as T ) as boolean
    if( this.st = nullptr ) then
        hBlockForever( )
        return FALSE
    end if

    dim as ChanState( of T ) ptr s = cptr( ChanState( of T ) ptr, this.st )
    MutexLock( s->core.lok )

    '' Send on a closed channel is a structural bug -- two parties disagree
    '' about who owns the producer role -- so it raises rather than returning
    '' FALSE, which would invite ignoring it.
    if( s->core.isclosed ) then
        MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
        error( FB.FB_RTERROR_ILLEGALFUNCTIONCALL )
        return FALSE
    end if

    '' 1. Direct handoff.  The value goes straight into the waiting receiver's
    ''    output variable and never enters the buffer -- see the file header.
    dim as ChanWaiterCore ptr w = hPopRecv( @s->core )
    if( w ) then
        *(cptr( ChanWaiter( of T ) ptr, w )->slot) = v
        w->closedwake = FALSE
        MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
        Unpark( w->parker )
        return TRUE
    end if

    '' 2. Buffer, if there is room.
    if( s->core.num < s->core.cap ) then
        s->buf( s->core.tail ) = v
        s->core.tail = ( s->core.tail + 1 ) mod s->core.cap
        s->core.num += 1
        MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
        return TRUE
    end if

    '' 3. Block.  'slot' points at the caller's own value, which stays alive
    ''    for the duration of this call.
    dim as ChanWaiter( of T ) w2
    w2.c.parker     = ParkerSelf( )
    w2.slot       = @v
    w2.c.claimed    = FALSE
    w2.c.closedwake = FALSE
    hPushSend( @s->core, @w2.c )
    MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )

    if( this.hWait( w2, TRUE, FALSE, 0 ) = FALSE ) then
        return FALSE            '' untimed: only reachable on a spurious wake
    end if

    if( w2.c.closedwake ) then
        error( FB.FB_RTERROR_ILLEGALFUNCTIONCALL )
        return FALSE
    end if

    return TRUE
end function

function Channel( of T ).TrySend( byref v as T ) as boolean
    if( this.st = nullptr ) then return FALSE

    dim as ChanState( of T ) ptr s = cptr( ChanState( of T ) ptr, this.st )
    MutexLock( s->core.lok )

    if( s->core.isclosed ) then
        MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
        error( FB.FB_RTERROR_ILLEGALFUNCTIONCALL )
        return FALSE
    end if

    dim as ChanWaiterCore ptr w = hPopRecv( @s->core )
    if( w ) then
        *(cptr( ChanWaiter( of T ) ptr, w )->slot) = v
        w->closedwake = FALSE
        MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
        Unpark( w->parker )
        return TRUE
    end if

    if( s->core.num < s->core.cap ) then
        s->buf( s->core.tail ) = v
        s->core.tail = ( s->core.tail + 1 ) mod s->core.cap
        s->core.num += 1
        MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
        return TRUE
    end if

    MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
    return FALSE
end function

function Channel( of T ).SendFor( byref v as T, byval ms as ulong ) as boolean
    if( this.st = nullptr ) then
        dim as any ptr p = ParkerSelf( )
        ParkTimed( p, ms )
        return FALSE
    end if

    dim as ChanState( of T ) ptr s = cptr( ChanState( of T ) ptr, this.st )
    MutexLock( s->core.lok )

    if( s->core.isclosed ) then
        MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
        error( FB.FB_RTERROR_ILLEGALFUNCTIONCALL )
        return FALSE
    end if

    dim as ChanWaiterCore ptr w = hPopRecv( @s->core )
    if( w ) then
        *(cptr( ChanWaiter( of T ) ptr, w )->slot) = v
        w->closedwake = FALSE
        MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
        Unpark( w->parker )
        return TRUE
    end if

    if( s->core.num < s->core.cap ) then
        s->buf( s->core.tail ) = v
        s->core.tail = ( s->core.tail + 1 ) mod s->core.cap
        s->core.num += 1
        MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
        return TRUE
    end if

    dim as ChanWaiter( of T ) w2
    w2.c.parker     = ParkerSelf( )
    w2.slot       = @v
    w2.c.claimed    = FALSE
    w2.c.closedwake = FALSE
    hPushSend( @s->core, @w2.c )
    MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )

    if( this.hWait( w2, TRUE, TRUE, ms ) = FALSE ) then
        return FALSE
    end if

    if( w2.c.closedwake ) then
        error( FB.FB_RTERROR_ILLEGALFUNCTIONCALL )
        return FALSE
    end if

    return TRUE
end function

'' ------------------------------------------------------------------ receive

function Channel( of T ).Receive( byref out as T ) as boolean
    if( this.st = nullptr ) then
        hBlockForever( )
        return FALSE
    end if

    dim as ChanState( of T ) ptr s = cptr( ChanState( of T ) ptr, this.st )
    MutexLock( s->core.lok )

    '' 1. Buffered item first, so a close never discards what was sent before
    ''    it and order is preserved when senders are also queued.
    if( s->core.num > 0 ) then
        out = s->buf( s->core.head )
        s->core.head = ( s->core.head + 1 ) mod s->core.cap
        s->core.num -= 1

        '' a queued sender now has room: move its value into the slot just
        '' vacated, rather than handing it to us out of order
        dim as ChanWaiterCore ptr w = hPopSend( @s->core )
        if( w ) then
            s->buf( s->core.tail ) = *(cptr( ChanWaiter( of T ) ptr, w )->slot)
            s->core.tail = ( s->core.tail + 1 ) mod s->core.cap
            s->core.num += 1
            w->closedwake = FALSE
            MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
            Unpark( w->parker )
            return TRUE
        end if

        MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
        return TRUE
    end if

    '' 2. Empty buffer, but a sender is waiting: rendezvous handoff.
    dim as ChanWaiterCore ptr w2 = hPopSend( @s->core )
    if( w2 ) then
        out = *(cptr( ChanWaiter( of T ) ptr, w2 )->slot)
        w2->closedwake = FALSE
        MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
        Unpark( w2->parker )
        return TRUE
    end if

    '' 3. Closed and drained is the end of the stream, not a failure.  'out' is
    ''    left untouched: FreeBASIC has no universal zero for a UDT, and leaving
    ''    the caller's variable alone is both cheaper and less surprising.
    if( s->core.isclosed ) then
        MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
        return FALSE
    end if

    '' 4. Block.
    dim as ChanWaiter( of T ) w3
    w3.c.parker     = ParkerSelf( )
    w3.slot       = @out
    w3.c.claimed    = FALSE
    w3.c.closedwake = FALSE
    hPushRecv( @s->core, @w3.c )
    MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )

    if( this.hWait( w3, FALSE, FALSE, 0 ) = FALSE ) then
        return FALSE
    end if

    return ( w3.c.closedwake = FALSE )
end function

function Channel( of T ).TryReceive( byref out as T ) as boolean
    if( this.st = nullptr ) then return FALSE

    dim as ChanState( of T ) ptr s = cptr( ChanState( of T ) ptr, this.st )
    MutexLock( s->core.lok )

    if( s->core.num > 0 ) then
        out = s->buf( s->core.head )
        s->core.head = ( s->core.head + 1 ) mod s->core.cap
        s->core.num -= 1
        dim as ChanWaiterCore ptr w = hPopSend( @s->core )
        if( w ) then
            s->buf( s->core.tail ) = *(cptr( ChanWaiter( of T ) ptr, w )->slot)
            s->core.tail = ( s->core.tail + 1 ) mod s->core.cap
            s->core.num += 1
            w->closedwake = FALSE
            MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
            Unpark( w->parker )
            return TRUE
        end if
        MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
        return TRUE
    end if

    dim as ChanWaiterCore ptr w2 = hPopSend( @s->core )
    if( w2 ) then
        out = *(cptr( ChanWaiter( of T ) ptr, w2 )->slot)
        w2->closedwake = FALSE
        MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
        Unpark( w2->parker )
        return TRUE
    end if

    MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
    return FALSE
end function

function Channel( of T ).ReceiveFor( byref out as T, byval ms as ulong ) as boolean
    if( this.st = nullptr ) then
        dim as any ptr p = ParkerSelf( )
        ParkTimed( p, ms )
        return FALSE
    end if

    dim as ChanState( of T ) ptr s = cptr( ChanState( of T ) ptr, this.st )
    MutexLock( s->core.lok )

    if( s->core.num > 0 ) then
        out = s->buf( s->core.head )
        s->core.head = ( s->core.head + 1 ) mod s->core.cap
        s->core.num -= 1
        dim as ChanWaiterCore ptr w = hPopSend( @s->core )
        if( w ) then
            s->buf( s->core.tail ) = *(cptr( ChanWaiter( of T ) ptr, w )->slot)
            s->core.tail = ( s->core.tail + 1 ) mod s->core.cap
            s->core.num += 1
            w->closedwake = FALSE
            MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
            Unpark( w->parker )
            return TRUE
        end if
        MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
        return TRUE
    end if

    dim as ChanWaiterCore ptr w2 = hPopSend( @s->core )
    if( w2 ) then
        out = *(cptr( ChanWaiter( of T ) ptr, w2 )->slot)
        w2->closedwake = FALSE
        MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
        Unpark( w2->parker )
        return TRUE
    end if

    if( s->core.isclosed ) then
        MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
        return FALSE
    end if

    dim as ChanWaiter( of T ) w3
    w3.c.parker     = ParkerSelf( )
    w3.slot       = @out
    w3.c.claimed    = FALSE
    w3.c.closedwake = FALSE
    hPushRecv( @s->core, @w3.c )
    MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )

    if( this.hWait( w3, FALSE, TRUE, ms ) = FALSE ) then
        return FALSE
    end if

    return ( w3.c.closedwake = FALSE )
end function

'' -------------------------------------------------------------------- close
''
'' One-way, and closing twice raises: a second close means two parties believe
'' they own the producer role.  Every blocked waiter is woken -- a close that
'' left waiters parked forever would be useless for shutdown, which is most of
'' what close is for.
sub Channel( of T ).Close( )
    if( this.st = nullptr ) then
        error( FB.FB_RTERROR_ILLEGALFUNCTIONCALL )
        exit sub
    end if

    dim as ChanState( of T ) ptr s = cptr( ChanState( of T ) ptr, this.st )
    MutexLock( s->core.lok )

    if( s->core.isclosed ) then
        MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
        error( FB.FB_RTERROR_ILLEGALFUNCTIONCALL )
        exit sub
    end if

    s->core.isclosed = TRUE

    '' Collect first, unpark after the lock is dropped: unparking under the
    '' lock would have every woken thread immediately block on it.
    dim as ChanWaiterCore ptr woken = nullptr
    dim as ChanWaiterCore ptr tail_ = nullptr

    do
        dim as ChanWaiterCore ptr w = hPopRecv( @s->core )
        if( w = nullptr ) then exit do
        w->closedwake = TRUE
        w->nextw = nullptr
        if( tail_ ) then tail_->nextw = w else woken = w
        tail_ = w
    loop

    do
        dim as ChanWaiterCore ptr w = hPopSend( @s->core )
        if( w = nullptr ) then exit do
        w->closedwake = TRUE
        w->nextw = nullptr
        if( tail_ ) then tail_->nextw = w else woken = w
        tail_ = w
    loop

    MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )

    while( woken )
        dim as ChanWaiterCore ptr nxt = woken->nextw
        Unpark( woken->parker )
        woken = nxt
    wend
end sub

'' ---------------------------------------------------------------- iteration
''
'' Drains until the channel is closed and empty, then ends.  Iterating a
'' channel nobody closes blocks forever, which is correct and worth stating
'' because it presents as a hang rather than as a design.

function Channel( of T ).ElemPtr( ) as T ptr
    return nullptr
end function

function Channel( of T ).GetIterator( ) as ChannelIterator( of T )
    dim as ChannelIterator( of T ) it
    it.src = @this
    it.ok  = this.Receive( it.cur )
    return it
end function

function ChannelIterator( of T ).IsValid( ) as boolean
    return this.ok
end function

function ChannelIterator( of T ).Value( ) as T
    return this.cur
end function

sub ChannelIterator( of T ).MoveNext( )
    dim as Channel( of T ) ptr c = cptr( Channel( of T ) ptr, this.src )
    this.ok = c->Receive( this.cur )
end sub

'' ------------------------------------------- typed commits, for SELECT
''
'' Called only from select.tiko, and only after SelectRun( ) polled this
'' channel ready with its lock HELD.  Each performs the typed transfer and then
'' UNLOCKS.  Releasing the lock before the transfer would let another thread
'' take the item between the poll and the commit, which is the whole reason the
'' poll happens under the lock.

function ChanCommitRecvLocked( of T ) _
    ( byref ch as Channel( of T ), byval dst as T ptr ) as boolean

    dim as ChanState( of T ) ptr s = cptr( ChanState( of T ) ptr, ch.Handle( ) )

    if( s->core.num > 0 ) then
        *dst = s->buf( s->core.head )
        s->core.head = ( s->core.head + 1 ) mod s->core.cap
        s->core.num -= 1

        '' a queued sender now has room: move its value into the vacated slot
        '' rather than handing it over out of order
        dim as ChanWaiterCore ptr w = hPopSend( @s->core )
        if( w ) then
            s->buf( s->core.tail ) = *(cptr( ChanWaiter( of T ) ptr, w )->slot)
            s->core.tail = ( s->core.tail + 1 ) mod s->core.cap
            s->core.num += 1
            w->closedwake = FALSE
            MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
            Unpark( w->parker )
            return TRUE
        end if

        MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
        return TRUE
    end if

    dim as ChanWaiterCore ptr w2 = hPopSend( @s->core )
    if( w2 ) then
        *dst = *(cptr( ChanWaiter( of T ) ptr, w2 )->slot)
        w2->closedwake = FALSE
        MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
        Unpark( w2->parker )
        return TRUE
    end if

    '' closed and drained: end of stream, 'dst' left untouched
    MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
    return FALSE
end function

function ChanCommitSendLocked( of T ) _
    ( byref ch as Channel( of T ), byval src as T ptr ) as boolean

    dim as ChanState( of T ) ptr s = cptr( ChanState( of T ) ptr, ch.Handle( ) )

    if( s->core.isclosed ) then
        MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
        error( FB.FB_RTERROR_ILLEGALFUNCTIONCALL )
        return FALSE
    end if

    dim as ChanWaiterCore ptr w = hPopRecv( @s->core )
    if( w ) then
        *(cptr( ChanWaiter( of T ) ptr, w )->slot) = *src
        w->closedwake = FALSE
        MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
        Unpark( w->parker )
        return TRUE
    end if

    if( s->core.num < s->core.cap ) then
        s->buf( s->core.tail ) = *src
        s->core.tail = ( s->core.tail + 1 ) mod s->core.cap
        s->core.num += 1
        MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
        return TRUE
    end if

    MutexUnlock( s->core.lok )
    return FALSE
end function

'' ------------------------------------------------- directional channels
''
'' RFC-0005 section 3.  One-way views, so a procedure signature can state which
'' end it takes and the compiler enforces it:
''
''     sub Producer( byval out as SendChan( of long ) )    '' cannot receive
''     sub Consumer( byval in_ as RecvChan( of long ) )    '' cannot send or Close
''
'' RecvChan deliberately has NO Close: closing is the producer's statement, and
'' a consumer that closes its input is the most common channel bug there is.
''
'' Each holds a Channel BY VALUE rather than re-implementing the refcount, so
'' copying, assignment and destruction are the Channel's own and cannot drift
'' from it.  Conversion is ONE WAY -- there is no cast back to Channel, because
'' a directional handle is a narrowing and narrowing should not be reversible by
'' accident.
''
'' These are ERGONOMICS, NOT SAFETY.  Nothing stops a caller keeping the full
'' Channel and using both ends; what they make checkable is the intent at the
'' boundary where it is written down.

public type SendChan( of T )
    private:
    as Channel( of T ) ch

    public:
    declare constructor( )
    declare constructor( byref c as Channel( of T ) )

    declare function Send   ( byref v as T ) as boolean
    declare function TrySend( byref v as T ) as boolean
    declare function SendFor( byref v as T, byval ms as ulong ) as boolean
    declare sub      Close( )
    declare function IsClosed( ) as boolean
    declare function IsNil( ) as boolean
    declare function Capacity( ) as long
end type

constructor SendChan( of T )( )
end constructor

constructor SendChan( of T )( byref c as Channel( of T ) )
    this.ch = c
end constructor

function SendChan( of T ).Send( byref v as T ) as boolean
    return this.ch.Send( v )
end function
function SendChan( of T ).TrySend( byref v as T ) as boolean
    return this.ch.TrySend( v )
end function
function SendChan( of T ).SendFor( byref v as T, byval ms as ulong ) as boolean
    return this.ch.SendFor( v, ms )
end function
sub SendChan( of T ).Close( )
    this.ch.Close( )
end sub
function SendChan( of T ).IsClosed( ) as boolean
    return this.ch.IsClosed( )
end function
function SendChan( of T ).IsNil( ) as boolean
    return this.ch.IsNil( )
end function
function SendChan( of T ).Capacity( ) as long
    return this.ch.Capacity( )
end function

public type RecvChan( of T )
    private:
    as Channel( of T ) ch

    public:
    declare constructor( )
    declare constructor( byref c as Channel( of T ) )

    declare function Receive   ( byref out as T ) as boolean
    declare function TryReceive( byref out as T ) as boolean
    declare function ReceiveFor( byref out as T, byval ms as ulong ) as boolean
    declare function IsClosed( ) as boolean
    declare function IsNil( ) as boolean
    declare function Count( ) as long
    declare function GetIterator( ) as ChannelIterator( of T )
end type

constructor RecvChan( of T )( )
end constructor

constructor RecvChan( of T )( byref c as Channel( of T ) )
    this.ch = c
end constructor

function RecvChan( of T ).Receive( byref out as T ) as boolean
    return this.ch.Receive( out )
end function
function RecvChan( of T ).TryReceive( byref out as T ) as boolean
    return this.ch.TryReceive( out )
end function
function RecvChan( of T ).ReceiveFor( byref out as T, byval ms as ulong ) as boolean
    return this.ch.ReceiveFor( out, ms )
end function
function RecvChan( of T ).IsClosed( ) as boolean
    return this.ch.IsClosed( )
end function
function RecvChan( of T ).IsNil( ) as boolean
    return this.ch.IsNil( )
end function
function RecvChan( of T ).Count( ) as long
    return this.ch.Count( )
end function
function RecvChan( of T ).GetIterator( ) as ChannelIterator( of T )
    return this.ch.GetIterator( )
end function

